% ============================================================================
% homophones — a common word and another word that sounds exactly the same
% (a homophone) but is spelled differently and means something different
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling library to `opposites.adj` and `synonyms.adj`: some words SOUND
% identical when spoken but are spelled differently and mean different
% things. THERE and THEIR sound the same but are not the same word. The word
% for "sounds the same but is spelled/means differently" is a HOMOPHONE.
% This tiny library records a handful of clean word→homophone pairs so an
% ADJ program can recall them, each one cited to a dictionary. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? homophone(there, $Word)      % their
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `homophone(word, sound_alike)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% sound-alike word WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% ABSTAINS on any word not in the table (it never invents a homophone it
% cannot cite).
%
% The pairs, as a truth table:
%
%     word     sound_alike   read as…
%     -------  ------------  ---------------------------------------
%     there    their         "there and their sound the same"
%     flower   flour         "flower and flour sound the same"
%     to       too           "to and too sound the same"
%
% Only ONE direction is shipped per pair (word → its sound-alike). A query
% like `? homophone(their, $W)` therefore ABSTAINS: the honest-abstention
% property is deliberate — the table answers only what its source explicitly
% lists, and a word with no shipped homophone in this table (e.g. `here`, a
% real word with a real homophone "hear" that this table does not carry)
% returns no invented answer. (This mirrors `opposites.adj`'s and
% `synonyms.adj`'s own one-direction convention.)
%
% Provenance — nothing here is from memory. Each pair is copied from the
% English Wiktionary entry for the FIRST word, whose "Homophones" line lists
% the sound-alike word verbatim. The per-pair citable spans (quoted exactly
% as printed) are:
%
%     there  → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/there   "Homophones: their, they're"
%     flower → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower  "Homophone: flour (for people who pronounce flower as
%                                              one syllable, or flour as two)"
%     to     → en.wiktionary.org/wiki/to      "Homophones: too, two"
%
% The single `source` span below is the clearest one (the `there` entry); the
% `locator` points at that entry, and each remaining pair's own page + verbatim
% span is quoted above so every row is independently checkable. `trust
% consensus` — a dictionary is a secondary reference, not a primary authority,
% the same tier `opposites.adj`/`synonyms.adj` already established for the
% same Wiktionary source. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table homophone {
    columns word, sound_alike

    row (there, their)
    row (flower, flour)
    row (to, too)

    source "Homophones: their, they're"
    locator "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/there"
    trust consensus
}
